        "ImageDescription": {
          "value": "The Nintendo 64 controller, shown from above.  The controller features an odd and unique design that is meant to be held in multiple ways.  Most commonly, however, the controller was held with the left hand on the middle prong and the right hand over the buttons.  Most games made use of the analog joystick for moving about in 3D space, which gave the d-pad little to do.  Gamepads would later evolve to have both a d-pad and analog stick on the left, removing the need for an awkward three-prong design.",
          "source": "commons-desc-page"
        },
